{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789897426,"nonce":"amGaJvQGpjEofx42O2q8W7oCUsc=","title":"Linux arm64: every object crossing the bridge faults, the pointer sign-extended from bit 47","message":"`caribou run hud.hl` and the interop bench die with SIGSEGV on ubuntu-24.04-arm (Nightly and Bench legs), and in an arm64 Ubuntu 24.04 container. gdb: the fault is `ldr x8, [x24]` in `bridge::invoke_named` with x24 = 0xffffffff1040e500, si_addr the same; the process's heap mapping is 0xffff04963000..0xffff8c7c0000, so the object is 0xffff1040e500 and the read address is that pointer sign-extended from bit 47.\n\n`caribou_abi::Value::as_object` did `((bits \u0026 PTR_MASK) \u003c\u003c 16) as i64 \u003e\u003e 16`. Linux on arm64 gives user space the full 48-bit range, so mmap returns addresses with bit 47 set; x86_64 Linux stays below 2^47 and macOS arm64 uses 47 bits, which is why only this platform saw it. wren_lift's `Value` reads its pointer with `bits \u0026 PTR_MASK` and no extension, and passes on the same runner.\n\nFix: read the pointer zero-extended. No layout change; the bits a plugin encodes are the same.","files":null}]}